.TH TIMECRATE\-GUI 1 "2026-11-11" "timecrate 3.2.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
timecrate\-gui \- graphical front-end for Timecrate backups
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timecrate\-gui
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timecrate\-gui
is a GTK4 and libadwaita front-end for
.BR timecrate (1).
It shows the live state of the backup \(em the archives held on the remote, when the last backup
ran, whether the encryption key has been escrowed and whether the last recovery drill passed \(em
and runs backups, restores and drills.
.PP
It is a front-end only. Every operation is carried out by
.BR timecrate (1),
whose output is shown verbatim in the activity log, and both read the same configuration.
Anything done here can be done from the command line, and a machine with no graphical session
loses no capability.
.PP
Operations that need root are run through
.BR pkexec (1),
so you are asked to authenticate each time. Cancelling that prompt cancels the operation and
changes nothing.
.SH RESTORING
Selecting an archive fetches it, verifies its checksum and signature, decrypts it and extracts it
into a staging directory for you to inspect. Your running system is not overwritten. Archives
written before 3.0.0 are listed and restored the same way.
.PP
Under
.BR "Checks and recovery" ,
a bare\-metal restore extracts the newest archive straight over
.I /
after the same signature check, and only once you type
.B RESTORE
to confirm; break\-glass recovers from an archive already on disk, into a folder you choose.
.SH REMOTE AND FOLDER
The
.B Remote and folder
page shows where backups are written, which account they go to, whether the authorisation is still
valid, whether the folder can be reached right now, and how much space the provider reports. From
it you can connect or re-authorise a provider, test that the folder can be both listed and written
to, browse the remote and choose a different folder, and make this machine forget the remote.
.PP
Choosing a folder does not take effect immediately: the backup count at both the current and the
new location is shown first, because repointing away from a folder that holds backups leaves them
in place and invisible. Forgetting a remote deletes nothing and does not revoke access at the
provider.
.PP
Besides Google Drive and Dropbox, the destination can be a
.B Folder on this computer or an attached drive
or
.BR "Another rclone remote" ,
one you already set up with
.BR "rclone config" .
A local folder must be writable by the account the backups run as, and one on the same disk does not
protect against that disk failing. It is shown as a folder, with no account or sign-in, and cannot
be forgotten; choose another destination instead.
.PP
.B Second copy
sends every backup to a second place as well, chosen the same two ways, or turns that off. It
changes nothing about the first destination.
.SH SCHEDULE
The
.B Schedule
page sets how often the backup and the recovery drill run. Besides the presets, the backup can run
.B Daily at a time you choose
(a 24\-hour HH:MM). Changes are applied when the settings window is closed, each with administrator
approval, even if nothing else in it changed.
.PP
.B Pause scheduled backups
skips the scheduled backups for 1, 3, 7 or 30 days; the recovery drill still runs. While a pause is
in force the main window says so at the top, with a button to resume.
.PP
If the scheduled runs are not yet tied to an account, the main window says so at the top: the
nightly run would otherwise act as root and fail to find the keys, although a backup started from
the window still works. The button there pins the account you are using.
.SH KEYS AND RECOVERY
The
.B Keys and recovery
page reports the encryption key, the signing key, whether their secret halves are on this
machine, and whether the escrow has been confirmed for the key actually in use. From it you can
import an escrowed key onto a rebuilt machine, check that this machine can read the backups,
export the key for escrow, write a printable recovery sheet, and record that a copy exists
off\-box. On a machine with no key at all, the window offers to restore an existing identity
before it offers to generate a new one \(em generating one where backups already exist would leave
them unreadable.
.PP
.B harden
is reported here but not offered: its safety check is an interactive off\-box comparison that a
process with no terminal cannot carry out, so a button could only skip it. Run it from
.BR timecrate (1).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/timecrate/timecrate.conf
System configuration, shared with the command-line tool.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/timecrate/status.json
The status this window renders.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timecrate (1),
.BR pkexec (1)
.PP
.I RESTORE.md
in
.IR /usr/share/doc/timecrate/
